										NETWORKREDUNDANCY
Network Redundancy
	Deploying critical network devices in redundant pairs instead of single device to eleminate single points of failure (backup device takes over if main device goes down)

Steps to implement network redundancy (router and switch redundancy)
	1) Deploy critical devices in redundant pairs
	2) Configure failover
		1) Layer 3
			> Network set up with static routes: admin must configure backup static routes through redundant device for if any directly connected device goes down
			> Network set up with dynamic routes: automatically handled by dynamic routing protocols (through backup routes in routing db)
		2) Layer 2
			Automatically handled by frame flooding and mac-address learning
	3) Handle issues caused due to redundancy
		1) Default gateways for end-hosts do not get automatically updated after LAN-edge router failover
			Solution Layer 2 & 3 -> FHRPs: automatic gateway failover (handles layer 3 failover internally)
		2) Loop formation due to redundant devices
			> Solution Layer 3 -> Automatic; 1) Inbult loop-prevention mechanisms in dynamic routing protocols 2) TTL field in IP header
			> Solution Layer 2 -> STP: automatically detects loops, blocks redundant links breaking loops (loop prevention), opens links if failure (failover at layer 2)

Load-balancing
	> Additional load-balancing can also be configured across the redundant pair for full utilisation
	> Admin configured load-balancing: Half traffic passes through 1st device (2nd device as backup), other half traffic passes through 2nd device (1st device as backup)
		=> Entire bandwidth is exhausted at all times

