-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------HSRP----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Network redundancy at layer 3
	> Failover & path selection
		> Statically routed network: Admin need to configure backup static routes via redundant device
		> Dynamically routed network: Automatically handled by dynamic routing protocols
		> Automatically handled by HSRP (FHRPs)
	> Issues due to redundant devices
		> Default gateways for end-hosts do not get automatically updated after LAN-edge router failover
			Solution -> HSRP (FHRPs): automatic gateway failover
		> Loop formation
			Solution -> Automatically handled due to 1) Inbuilt loop prevention mechanism in dynamic routing protocols 2) TTL field in the IP header

FHRPs (First Hop Redundancy Protocols)
	> Problem Addressed
		> Static default gateway configuration for end-hosts (Standard failovers dont update default gateway information configured at end-hosts)
	> Automatic gateway failover (internally handles failover at router pair + makes sure active router is configured at end-hosts as default gaetway)
		> Combines the LAN-edge router pair into a single virtual router with a virtual IP (VIP) and a virtual MAC (VMAC)
		> Elects from the router pair an active (handles entire traffic) and a standby (takes over if active goes down) => Automatic failover at LAN-edge router pair
		> End-hosts use this VIP (virtual IP) as their default gateway address => Automatic failover for default gateway configuration at end-host
	> Automatic Load balancing capability varies for each protocol, depends on the internal router pair deployment configuration:
		1) Active/standby - No native load balancing
		2) Active/active - Native load balancing
	> Common FHRPs:
		> HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol): Cisco proprietary, most common, active/standby router pair deployment - No native load balancing
		> VRRP: Open standard, active/standby router pair deployment - No native load balancing
		> GLBP: Cisco proprietary, active/active router pair deployment - Native load balancing

HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol)
	> Comibes the router pair to a virtual router (VIP & VMAC), used as default gateway for end-hosts, automatically provides failover at router pair
	> Deploys router pair as Active/standby pair => No native load balancing
	> HSRP Operations
		> Both routers have their normal physical IP and MAC addresses on their HSRP interface
		> Both routers also have the HSRP VIP and VMAC addresses on their HSRP interface
		> When both router come online, and HSRP is enabled on both routers, one is elected as the active router and the other remains as the standby router
		> Active router owns the VIP and VMAC addresses => responds to arp requests, all traffic addressed for the VIP address goes through the active router
		> Routers send hello messages to each other through their HSRP enabled interface
		> If the standby router stops recieving hello messages from the actve router, it transitions from standby to active, takes ownership of VIP and VMAC
	> HSRP is configured on the interface level of a router
	> HSRP Configuration
		> cmd: (config-if)#standby <groupNumber> ip <vip>
		> groupNumber
			> specifies the HSRP virtual router # in the VLAN
			> must match across routers forming the HSRP redundant pair
			> locally significant to the subnet/VLAN
			> routers share their group number with each other in hello messages, if different group numbers => hello msgs ignored => routers dont find each other
		> vip
			> ip address to be used as the vip address
	> HSRP Priority
		> Active router gets elected based on its priority
		> cmd: (config-if)#standby 1 priority <priorityValue>
		> Higher priority is preferred
		> Default is 100
		> In case of priority tie, higher IP address wins
	> HSRP Pre-emption
		> If pre-emption enabled, an HSRP router with higher priority once comes/comes back up, will transition/transition back to being active
		> cmd: (config-if)#standby 1 preempt
		> Less stable <= The higher priority router might keep flapping up-down due to an issue, continuous switching of the active router
		> If pre-emption not enbled (default), lower priority router will remain as active router when a higher priority router come back online
	> HSRP Version
		> HSRPv2
		> HSRPv1 (default)
		> Router pair must run same HSRP version in order to recognize each other <= Format of hello message different in both versions
	> HSRP configuration mismatch
		> group number, vip, and hsrp version, all must match across routers to form an hsrp redundant router pair
		> group number mismatch: routers recognize but ignore each other
		> vip mismatch: hsrp peering cannot be fully completed, error is thrown on console "virtual ip mismatch"
		> hsrp version mismatch: routers dot understand each other <= format of hello message is different across versions
	> Manual Load balancing implementation with HSRP
		1) Load balancing within subnet
			> Aim: have 50% traffic through R1 & other 50% traffic through R2
			> 2 HSRP router groups are configured out of the same router pair in the same subnet, Grp1[R1(Active)/R2(Standby)] Grp2[R2(Active)/R1(Standby)]
				=> Both routers managed to be kept active at the same time, by having each own a different VIP
			> 50% hosts are configured for Grp1 as default gateway, rest 50% hosts are configured for Grp2 as default gateway
				=> 50% traffic through R1 & other 50% traffic through R2
		2) Load balancing across subnets
			> Aim: have a subnets's traffic through R1 & other subnet's traffic through R2
			> An HSRP router group (R1:active) is configured for subnet1 & another HSRP router group (R2:active) is configured for subnet2
				=> R1 active gateway (R2 standby gateway) for subnet1 & R2 active gateway (R1 standby gateway) for subnet2

