-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------STP-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Network redundancy at Layer 2
	Failover and path selection
		> Automatically handled by frame flooding and mac-address learning
		> Automatically handled by STP
	Issues due to redundant devices
		> Lack of default gateway updation after layer 3 failover
			Solution -> HSRP (FHRPs): automatic gateway failover (already handled at layer 3, solves problem entirely, at layer 2 & 3)
		> Loop formation
			Solution -> STP: automatically detects loops, blocks links to break loops (loop prevention), unblocks links if case of failure (failover at layer 2)

Loop formation issue at layer 2
	R1	R2
	|	|
	CD1-----CD2	1) PC1 sends arp request for default gateway to Acc3
	|  \ /	|	2) Acc3 broadcasts it to CD1 and CD2
	|  / \	|	3) CD1 broadcasts it to R1 and CD2		// CD2 recieves same arp request 2x
	Acc3	Acc4	4) CD2 broadcasts it to CD1 and Acc4		// CD1 recieves same arp request 2x
	|	|			.						.
	PC1	PC2			.						.
					.						.
								|
								V
							
		Due to loops being formed in a redundant device switched network (w/o STP), the arp request doubles (sometimes triples) with each broadcast
						=> BROADCAST STORM => Chokes Network

								|
								V

		Redundant links leading to loop formation in a redundant switched network need to be blocked, to break loops
								+
				In case of failure, blocked links must open up to provide failover

								|
								V

					STP (Spanning Tree Protocol)
						1) Blocks redundant links, breaking loops => Loop prevention
						2) Unblocks the redundant links in case the corresponding active links go down => Failover

STP (Spanning Tree Protocol)
	> Provides loop prevention and failover in a redundant switched network
	> Load balancing
		> No load balancing possible within a single spanning tree instance
		> Load balancing possible if users split into VLANs, complementing loads can put on switches for each VLAN
	> STP is industry standard, by default, enabled on all vendor's switches

STP Process
	> When a switch first comes up, all ports will be in blocking state (no end-host traffic forward)
	> All ports will send out BPDUs to neighbour switches
	> BPDUs
		> Bridge Protocol Data Units: contain STP related information of sender switch, to be communicated with directly connected neighbours
		> BPDU contents: 1)switch's Bridge ID 2)local path cost to Root Bridge 3)local port id 4)timers
		> Bridge ID
			> Uniquely identifies the switch on the LAN
			> Bridge ID = switch's MAC address + admin defined Bridge Priority value
			> Bridge Priority value
				> Lower Bridge Priority value is preferred
				> 0 (highest priority) - 65535 (least priority)
				> Default: 32768
			> Bridge MAC address
				Lower MAC address is preferred
	> Task 1: Root Bridge Election, through communication with each other through BPDUs
	> Root Bridge
		> Master switch of the LAN
		> Spanning tree is built with Root Bridge on the top
		> All switches forward their traffic only towards the Root Bridge
		> Switch with best Bridge ID (least Bridge Priority value, least MAC address)
	> Task 2: Switches select Root Port and Root Path, through communication with each other through BPDUs
	> STP link cost
		> Link cost = ceiling(reference bandwidth / link bandwidth) => Higher bandwidth links have lower cost (for all bw > ref bw, link cost=1 (min))
		> Reference bandwidth
			1) Short-mode cost: 20 gbps, default
			2) Long-mode cost: 20 tbps, yeilds more precise costs (If you enable long-mode, make sure to enable it on all switches in LAN)
		> Manually set link cost (not recommended)
			#int f0/1
			#spanning-tree cost <costValue>
	> Root Path and Root Port calculation
		> Root Path of a switch
			> Out of all possible paths from switch to Root Bridge, the path with the least cumulative STP link cost
			> Each switch has only 1 Root Path
		> Root Port of a switch
			> Switch's exit interface on its Root Path
			> Each switch will have only 1 Root Port
		> Root Bridge advertises a Root Path cost of 0 in it BPDUs to neighbours
		> Switches are aware of costs of links to directly connected neighbours
		> Switches, to the recieved path cost, add cost of link the BPDU was recieved on, finding out the path's cost from itself to Root Bridge
		> Path with least cumulative STP link cost to Root Bridge is selected as the switch's Root Path, exit interface on Root Path as Root Port
		> This information of Root - Path, Cost, and Port is shared further down with neighbours, thru BPDUs, for them to calculate their Root Path, etc
		> This process continues till no new paths are recieved by switches in BPDUs => Root Path and Root Port are finalised
		> If multiple equal STP link cost paths recieved by a switch
			1) Path to uplinking switch with lower Switch ID/Bridge ID is Root Path
			2) If equal Bridge ID, path going to port on uplinking switch with lower Port ID (Port Priority + Port Number) is Root Path
			3) If equal Port ID, path going out of port on this switch with lower Port ID is Root Path
	> Root Ports transition to forwarding, and switches start forwarding user traffic out Root Ports, along Root Paths, towards Root Bridge
	> Task 3: Switches select their Designated Ports
		Designated Ports
			> Ports opposite to Root Ports on links are Designated Ports
			> Designated Ports point away from the Root Bridge, eaxctly opposite of Root Ports
			> All ports on the Root Bridge are Designated Ports
	> At this point, Root Path links have been fully configured by STP, Root Ports on one side, Designated Ports on other side
	> Task 4: Remaining links get configured as redundant
		Non Root Path links are redundant and get blocked by STP for loop prevention
		LOOP PREVENTION...
		1) Ports at both ends of this redundant link become designated initially => No end-host traffic but not completely blocked yet
		2) Switches at both ends send BPDUs to each other
		3) Switch sending better BPDUs to other (lower Root Path Cost, else lower Bridge ID) will have its port on that redundant link remain as Designated Port
		4) Other switch that sent worse BPDU will have its port on that redundant link transtion to Blocked Port (no outgoing traffic, incoming traffic allowed)
		5) Now Designated Port switch continues sending its BPDUs to the Blocked switch
		6) Blocked Port switch now only receives the superior BPDU, it does not send its BPDU or any traffic at all
		Redundant links are setup such that if Root Path failure, links making up next least cost path transition to forwarding for failover, becomes new Root Path
		FAILOVER...
		7) If incase Designated Port/better BPDU switch loses it Root Path, it no more sends better BPDUs to other switch
		8) Blocked Port switch, seeing worse BPDUs has its previously Blocked Port transition to Designated Port, starts sending its BPDUs to other switch
		9) 1st switch, learns about 2nd switch's Root Path, since it is the best path available for 1st, 1st makes the advertised path as its Root Path
		10) Designated Port on 1st transitions to Root Port, Blocked Port on 2nd transitions to Designated Port
		11) Hence if any link on any Root Path goes down, STP will transition appropriate links from redundant/blocked to forwarding

STP Versions (Open Standards)
	1) 802.1D STP
		> Original implementation of STP
		> Slow convergence time - 50s
		> 1 spanning tree instance for all VLANs in LAN
	2) 802.1w Rapid STP
		> RSTP
		> Faster convergence time - 10s
		> 1 spanning tree instance for all VLANs in LAN
	3) 802.1s Multiple STP
		> MSTP
		> Faster convergence time - 10s
		> Enables grouping and mapping VLANs into different spanning tree instances for load balancing

Load balancing with 802.1s MSTP
	> Users split into 20 VLANs at access layer
	> 2 spanning tree instances (VLANs 1...10 grouped into one spanning tree instance, VLANs 11...20 into other)
	> 1 redundant core-distribution switch pair
	> CD1 Root Bridge for VLAN1...10 => traffic forwarded on link to CD1, blocked on link to CD2
	> CD2 Root Bridge for VLAN11...20 => traffic forwarded on link to CD2, blocked on link to CD1
	> Load is split into half and divided between CD switches => Both switches operate at same time
	> In case of failure, entire load will shift to a single CD switch

STP Versions (Cisco Proprietary)
	1) Per VLAN Spanning Tree Plus (PVST+)
		> Enhanced implementation of 802.1D
		> Separate spanning tree instance for each VLAN
		> Slow convergence time, 50s
	2) Rapid Per VLAN Spanning Tree Plus (RPVST+)
		> Enhanced implementation of 802.1w
		> Separate spanning tree instance for each VLAN
		> Faster convergence time, 10s
	3) No cisco implementation for 802.1s => Cisco doesnt allow grouping multiple VLANs into single spanning tree instance
	> Blocked Ports are called Alternate Ports in cisco's STP implementation

Load balancing with PVST+/RPVST+
	> Users split into 20 VLANs 1...20
	> 20 spanning tree instances, each for a VLAN, since grouping is not allowed with cisco STPs
	> For 1...10 spanning tree instances, respectively for 1...10 VLANs, CD1 makes Root Bridge
	> For 11...20 spanning tree instances, respectively for 11...20 VLANs, CD2 makes Root Bridge
	> Load is split into 2 halves, distributed equally between both CD switches

Cisco STP version configuration on switch
	> cmd: (config)#spanning-tree mode <"mst" | "pvst" | "rapid-pvst">
	> pvst & rapid-pvst STP modes have exactly same configuration => Both modes are interchangable at any point, configuration will be preserved and will be in effect
	> switching STP mode to or from mst will have you lose previous configuration

Manupulating Root Bridge Election
	> Since STP has all switches send their traffic towards Root Bridge, Root Bridge becomes the centre of LAN
	> Only a pair of high-end core switches must be chosen as 1st and 2nd most preferred Root Bridge
	> Root Bridge election is based on Bridge IDs
		> Bridge ID = Bridge Priority value (admin configured, default 32768) + MAC address of switch
		> Switch with least Bridge Priority value, if tie, least MAC address (oldest switch) is elected as Root Bridge
	> Root Bridge election can be manupulated by explicitly setting Bridge Priority value
	> Since Bridge Priority value is set to default for all, switch with lowest MAC address => Oldest switch => Least performative switch will be elected as Root Bridge
	> Explicitly set Bridge priority value
		Using keywords
			> cmd: (config)#spanning-tree vlan 1 root primary	// internally sets the Bridge Priority value to 24576; 1st most preferred for Root Bridge
			> cmd: (config)#spanning-tree vlan 1 root secondary	// internally sets the Bridge Priority value to 28672; 2nd most preferred for Root Bridge
		Using exact values
			> cmd: (config)#spanning-tree vlan 1 priority <priorityValue>
			> Valid priority values: 0-61440
			> Values must increment by 4096
			> Values not multiples of 4096 or outside 0-61440 will return error message

HSRP and STP alignment
	> HSRP and STP configurations must align such that a directly connected router core-switch pair is selected as HSRP active and Root Bridge
	> Advantage of having HSRP active router directly connected to Root Bridge is that traffic will travel through most direct path instead of having intermediary hops

Portfast
	> When a port first comes up, it remains blocked for 30s, so STP can confirm it wont form any loops, before transiting to forwarding
	> Although only trunk ports (links connecting 2 switches) can be potentially loop forming, due to broadcast forwarding feature of switches
	> Access ports (ports linked to end-hosts) are non loop forming (since end-hosts dont forward broadcast traffic) => They can transition to forwarding immediately
	> Enabling Portfast on a port has it transtion to forwarding immediately, and have STP run checks after
	> Portfast configuration
		1) Interface level: (config-if)#spanning-tree portfast
		2) Global-switch level: (config)#spanning-tree portfast default
	> Portfast should only be enabled on ports that you are sure are non loop forming
	> Portfast should typically only be enabled on access ports, trunk ports are typically connected to other switches, are potentially loop forming, shouldnt have portfast
	> Portfast configuration on trunk ports require additional "trunk" keyword in cmd: (config-if)#spanning-tree portfast trunk // misconfiguration failsafe

Portfast Edge
	> Available on newer switches
	> Portfast Edge enabled on port
		> Port transitions to forwarding immediately (similar to Portfast)
		> In case BPDU received on port, port loses its Portfast Edge status, becomes normal STP port (unlike Portfast+BPDUGuard, where port gets disabled/shutdown)

BPDU Guard
	> BPDU Guard enabled on a Portfast enabled port prevents unknown/mistake replacement of end-host by another switch => Loops => Storm occurs before STP can take it down
	> BPDU Guard is used in conjunction with Portfast, BPDU Guard must always also be enabled along with Portfast
	> BPDU Guard looks for incoming BPDUs on port with Portfast, if BPDU received => End-host been replaced with a switch, BPDU Guard error disables (shuts) port immediately
	> Recover a BPDU error disabled port
		> Manual
			1) Correct issue on network
			2) Shutdown interface
			3) No shutdown interface
		> Automatic error disable recovery
			(config)#errdisable recovery cause bpduguard	// set automatic recovery on error disabled by cause bpdu guard
			(config)#errdisable recovery interval 30	// set recovery attempt interval to 30s
			> Not recommended, since untill network issue corrected, port will keep flapping

Root Guard
	> Prevents switches from unintentionally becoming the Root Bridge
	> Configured on switches, on interfaces pointing away from Root Bridge
	> Root Guard when enabled on a port, port transitions to 'root-inconsistent' and gets blocked if BPDUs superior than the current Root Bridge are received
  	> Since Root Guard enabled on interfaces facing outwards from Root, external switch attempting to become Root will have its BPDUs get blocked on the recieving end of link
	> Recovery: Automatic, nnce superior BPDUs stop coming in, port transitons through normal STP states

BPDU Filter
	> Can be enabled on 2 levels
		> Global-switch level
			> Applies to Portfast enabled intefaces, has similar effect to Portfast Edge
			> Sends initial BPDUs, then stops sending
			> If a BPDU is recieived, removes Portfast, disables BPDU Filtering, port acts as a normal STP port
		> Local-interface level
			> Port will not send BPDUs
			> Port will ignore incoming BPDUs
			> In effect, disabled STP
			> Useful when switch connected to a legacy switch (low MAC address) with single link (non loop forming position)
				legacy switches are not very configurable, and might cause issues with STP due to its low MAC address
					STP can be disabled on port connected to legacy switch by enabling interface level BPDU filter at port

Loop Guard
	> Unidirectional link failure
		> Cisco switches support GBIC/SFP modular ports
		> These ports take copper or fiber transreceiver and are typically used for switch to switch connections
		> Fiber cables have one strand for one-way data transfer, another for other-way data transfer
		> If one strand fails => Unidirectional link failure
		> Although interface will still show as up/up, but data transfer will only be possible in single direction
	> One-way loop caused by unidirectional link failure
		> On a Designated port - Blocked Port link, if unidirectional link from Designated to Blocked fails
		> Designated can no more send BPDUs to Blocked port
		> Blocked Port believes Designated Port has gone down
		> Blocked Port transitions to Designated Port itself and starts sending BPDUs of its own
		> Link has now transitoned to one-way forwarding instead of Blocked (blocked on other direction due to unidirectional link failure)
		> One-way loop formed
	> 2 solutions to solving unidirectional link problem
		1) Loop Guard
			> Spannig tree feature available in MST, PVST+, & RPVST+
		2) UDLD (Unidirectional Link Detection) protocol
			> Layer 2 protocol, not a part of spanning-tree
	> Loop Guard
		> BPDUs are expected to be received on Blocked and Root Ports
		> Loop Guard takes effect only on non-Designated Ports (Blocked & Root)
		> Loop Guard, even if enabled, does not affect Designated Ports at all, since they are not meant to receive BPDUs
		> If BPDUs are not received on Blocked or Root Ports, it will be put in 'Loop-Inconsistent' state instead of transitioning to Designated Port => All traffic blocked
		> Apart from unidirectional link failures, Loop Guard also protects from software failures and data corruption
		> Recovery: Automatic, port transitons to normal STP port once BPDUs are received again
		> Loop Guard configuration
			> Local-interface level
				(config-if)#spanning-tree guard loop
			> Global-switch level
				(config)#spanning-tree loopguard default	// enables loop guard on all interfaces, but takes no effect on Designated Ports
		> Loop Guard and Root Guard
			> Root Guard prevents undesired switches from becoming the Root Bridge
			> Root Guard prevents Designated Ports from becomimng non-Designated Root Ports
			> Loop Guard prevents unnidirectional link failures from causing loops
			> Loop Guard prevents Non-Designated (Root & Blocked) Ports from becoming Designated Ports
			> Root Guard and Loop Guard are exclusive features on ports, both cannot be enabled on same port at same time, enabling one will disable other on that port
			> For using Root and Loop guard on same switch, make sure to enable on different port individually, global config will entirely disable to other

STP, PVST+ vs RSTP, RPVST+, MST
	> STP
		Inbuilt: Single STP instance for all VLANs, timer-based convergence (slow)
	> PVST+
		Inbuilt: Separate STP instance for all VLANs, timer-based convergence (slow)
		Aditional features: Uplinkfast & Backbonefast (faster convergence by bypassing timers), portfast, loop guard, root guard, etc
	> RSTP
		Inbuilt: Single STP instance for all VLANs, faster convergence equivalent to Uplinkfast & Backbonefast built-in
	> RPVST+
		Inbuilt: Separate STP instance for all VLANs, faster convergence equivalent to Uplinkfast & Backbonefast built-in
		Additional features: same as PVST+
	> MST
		Inbuilt: VLANs can be grouped into STP instances, faster convergence equivalent to Uplinkfast & Backbonefast built-in

Convergence Mechanisms
	1) Timer based mechanism
		> Used in STP, PVST+ (default on most cisco switches)
		> Port goes through transitional states each for a certain time
		1) Blocked: max. 20s
		2) Listening: Transitional state, can send/receive BPDUs, does not forward traffic, does not learn mac addresses, 15s
		3) Learning: Transitional state, learns mac addresses, does not forward traffic, 15s
		4) Forwarding: Forwards traffic
		> Total time to reach forwarding state (20+15+15) = 50s
		> Portfast skips listening and learning states, port goes from Blocked to Forwarding within 20s (instead of 50s)
	2) Proactive Proposal/Agreement Sync mechanism
		> Used in RSTP, RPVST+, MSTP
		> Can be enabled in PVST+ through enabling Uplinkfast, and Backbonefast
		> Does not use timers, instead uses a proactive proposal/aggreement sync mechanism to rapidly build the spanning tree and respond to topology changes
		> Switches actively negotiate directly with each other that ports can safely transition to forwarding state, without need for timers
		> Convergence is achieved within few seconds across the topology

STP Version Interoperability
	> Switches may run different STP versions on the netwrok (not recommended)
	> Newer STP versions (RPVST+, MST) are backwards compatible with older STP version
	> Falling back to using older version for compatibility on a newer STP version running swicth happends on port level
	> If a switch running a newer version finds neighbour switch running older version, it'll shift itself on older version only on corresponding port for compatibility
	> On switching to older version, fast convergence will not be available on that link

RPVST+ and Hub Interoperability
	> Hubs
		> Layer 1 device, mac-address unaware
		> Received frame is flooded out all ports except one received on
		> Hubs have no memory => they can handle only 1 frame at a time
		> Hubs support only half duplex links => packets can only go in one direction at a time on links
	> Switches
		> Layer 2 devices => learn mac-addresses and send packets unicast
		> Switches have memory => multiple frames are handled at same time
		> Switches support full duplex links => 2-way packet transfer on links at same time
	> Switches, with other switches form full duplex links => RPVST+ (fast convergence) available
	> Switches, with hubs, form only half duplex links => RPVST+ (fast convergence) not available
	> RSTP, RPVST+, & MSTP offer an additional port role than 802.1D, & PVST+, a 'Backup' port role in Discarding/Blocking state
		> Alternate port (Non-designated), in Blocking state, on a switch is standby port for that switch's Root port
		> Backup port, in Blocking state, on a switch is standby port for that switch's Designated port on a shared segment (i.e Designated & Backup port linked to hub)
		> On using 802.1D, on using 'show' cmd on a switch, there will be 2 blocked ports shown, no way to distinguish which is standby of Root and which for Designated port
		> On using RSTP, 'show' cmd, port w 'Alternate' role standby for Root port, port w 'Backup' role standby for Designated port (when Designated & Backup linked to hub)
	

		

